#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157113 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/id #uuid "78635415-4c41-5fd4-8758-ed522da90e01" :question/math-academy-id "e-7918" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =(y + x)^3.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(1,1)$\n\n- $(-4,4)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that $f'(c)=0.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =(0+0)^{3} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1+1)^{3} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-4,4)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(-4,4)} \\\\  & =(4+(-4))^{3} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157117 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "2e9d5626-bb05-5382-91f4-efa0235b09be" :question/math-academy-id "e-7923" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 4y - e^{3x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y-{e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4(4y-{e}^{3x})-3{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =16y-7{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(16y-7{e}^{3x})|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =16(1)-7{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157118 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "ce6ca955-28f1-5912-bd36-dc6ef7be1241" :question/math-academy-id "e-7924" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 - 4 - y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(2,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n\n- $(2,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n\n- $(2,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(2,0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,0)} & ={({x}^{2}-4-y)|}_{(2,0)} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4-(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-4-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2x-({x}^{2}-4-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+2x+4+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(2,0)} & ={(-{x}^{2}+2x+4+y)|}_{(2,0)} \\\\  & =-(2)^{2}+2(2)+4+(0) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(2,0)} = 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(2,0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n\\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}  \\right|_{(2,0)}  > 0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424858ef-f37a-5808-83a0-9890b98fcc15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294949} {:answer/id #uuid "494f5b83-f79c-5c64-8d44-cb4acd239e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294950} {:answer/id #uuid "c51243e6-307a-5d0f-a9c4-8bae47e147c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294951} {:answer/id #uuid "60935ccf-d0bc-5fe1-a025-b66601ae718c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294952} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff4395e-0335-55ec-be9a-e6cd945a84e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "424858ef-f37a-5808-83a0-9890b98fcc15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294949} :answer-field/id #uuid "be3b760c-3192-5da6-a07f-6744370dba67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6089ab97-a271-5649-93dc-4070bae89004" :question/math-academy-id "q-163812" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$\n- $(\\pi ,-1)$\n- $(0,-1)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(0,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(0,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c98340b0-2c80-52f4-8b52-15cc2a7bed10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c98340b0-2c80-52f4-8b52-15cc2a7bed10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294963} :answer-field/id #uuid "61dcfd99-bd51-5e8e-ab25-09118a55d37f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d17faf10-2df9-589e-89d9-b965765aefa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-352601" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Given that the point $(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate ${y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)}.$\n\n${y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & =(2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =(2{y}^{2})^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =4y{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute $(2{y}^{2})^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =4y{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =4y(2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =8{y}^{3}+4y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)} & =(8{y}^{3}+4y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x})\\,{|}_{(1,-1)} \\\\  & =8(-1)^{3}+4(-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6e2fd80-9df5-5a3d-a5b0-b1d5eedfeb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156749} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe9616a-1215-5c05-86d7-d819eb888ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156750} {:answer/id #uuid "1b14361b-3b5b-50d8-a274-a353e692828b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156751} {:answer/id #uuid "45cd04e0-4e5a-5dad-b945-b8b246168cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156752} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4e327d-41af-5ec5-93ae-e1fdf9d65ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b4e327d-41af-5ec5-93ae-e1fdf9d65ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156753} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b1060dd-4804-5e1f-bd2b-fa1d02eeaa40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79579563-ab18-517d-b26e-0f91d0999c25" :question/math-academy-id "q-89414" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-y={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(-1,-2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(1,-2)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-y={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(-1,-2)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(-1,-2)} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}+(1) \\\\  & =5 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,-2)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(1,-2)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7b84c48-57e7-5309-93a4-57df7415b9cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156761} {:answer/id #uuid "0f33ac4f-09a2-5333-a946-583e33b73071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156762} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebc04dd-204b-5e91-8665-360f8c68aebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156763} {:answer/id #uuid "4bfb50ea-6795-5c27-bd34-f40299539d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156764} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd089ea-c220-5695-ab59-285fca9d09db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fd089ea-c220-5695-ab59-285fca9d09db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156765} :answer-field/id #uuid "f979375a-8d1f-51f7-95db-828c48043698" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156760}] :question/id #uuid "43029024-9f66-5703-b3dd-2d9243824692" :question/math-academy-id "q-89425" :question/problem "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation $y^{′} = y^{2} + x^{2}$. Given that the point $(1, 1)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of $y^{″} \\mid _{(1, 1)}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9089932e-cd41-58fd-acfe-0ebd086022f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156767} {:answer/id #uuid "03dcddb0-3b7d-51ec-803d-1dbb35f63138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156768} {:answer/id #uuid "603d717e-8b2d-5da3-a073-ba41d6195c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156769} {:answer/id #uuid "61da494e-3f94-5a72-a73f-e9c985c6ec73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156770} {:answer/id #uuid "651b6a42-39f2-5453-9ff5-be638666ff9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "603d717e-8b2d-5da3-a073-ba41d6195c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156769} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d0fcc1-d530-5848-89be-5d63ba24554a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156766}] :question/id #uuid "71ecf34a-b807-5183-a2fd-953c773f847a" :question/math-academy-id "q-89428" :question/problem "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2x^{3} - y$.\n\nGiven that the point $(1, 2)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(1, 2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n2. $(1, 2)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n3. $(1, 2)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76e28705-8b20-5ae5-94dc-898b9206bb76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294973} {:answer/id #uuid "70b9c44d-0ba5-5f11-a1d9-dd3fd54a0d1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294974} {:answer/id #uuid "927d8988-a753-5489-9f9e-e2de688f3338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294975} {:answer/id #uuid "47be7ca8-115b-55b8-bef8-75e79d48472d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294976} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ce2f34-520d-559b-a530-4fe705f77f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47be7ca8-115b-55b8-bef8-75e79d48472d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294976} :answer-field/id #uuid "ede1fd09-aa5b-5b2e-b309-1204551b67d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abdb69a3-efbc-5b10-a499-480d3008bd55" :question/math-academy-id "q-89869" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(2,2)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=y(x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)(1-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(4,-2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(4,-2)} \\\\  & =(-2)(4-(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(2,2)} \\\\  & =(2)(2-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0459cd9-b0c9-5a2b-a3fe-0c34023ea426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294980} {:answer/id #uuid "7e58f12c-a8c5-5b91-af02-8deb2a00b47d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294981} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef4eb47-96ad-5ba3-bbbd-2525c79b68cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294982} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4f3f21-2531-5da4-875a-f2b7caf92a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the points are the stationary points of the function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294983} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5b8333-baf9-5528-afbc-3fcb372ef6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c4f3f21-2531-5da4-875a-f2b7caf92a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the points are the stationary points of the function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294983} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c690bf6-5c44-5c59-b981-48b1409ff4c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "602325df-4815-541b-a3da-c6bd15c965f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-89871" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(2,-2)$\n- $(e,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,0)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(1,0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,-2)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(2,-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{4} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(e,1)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(e,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the points are the stationary points of the function.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df2e10d0-2bbc-5bd2-a690-6e9f506628fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294987} {:answer/id #uuid "779a4d2b-f73e-5068-ad16-7cff64866df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294988} {:answer/id #uuid "7a731647-7208-57b0-be91-2270bc83435b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294989} {:answer/id #uuid "b744c5f0-5bc2-59aa-9e90-982eda1bd145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294990} {:answer/id #uuid "697ac1ab-71bd-56eb-b4d7-140c97f7e720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "697ac1ab-71bd-56eb-b4d7-140c97f7e720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294991} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea3eef45-2156-5c18-ba47-fe189467b6c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f555eba5-d8ed-5c9b-af82-7f02d6c04aab" :question/math-academy-id "q-89879" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,2)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,2)} & ={(y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(0,2)} \\\\  & =2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35a3de20-a5fe-58ce-b37f-4d485d1c4a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295008} {:answer/id #uuid "e48b2108-d447-55f3-bb02-25d60925b04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295009} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba1838f-1cdd-5d0f-b46f-4c9717aed133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295010} {:answer/id #uuid "66a2b2f9-2a24-52c3-ab17-f2bd42ca9d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295011} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fd07c6-bb35-5096-9fb0-f14a20e5fe92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9fd07c6-bb35-5096-9fb0-f14a20e5fe92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295012} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbebe186-8810-5c73-9920-d84cdb9bbc9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e2017d38-ca7e-5dff-a660-bcc0c1d7b463" :question/math-academy-id "q-89887" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\pi ,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\pi ,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+0-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)}=1\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\pi ,1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(\\pi ,1),$ therefore we cannot have a local maximum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\pi ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa871edb-c514-5d5b-8153-0f2e02e75a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295015} {:answer/id #uuid "02cbe4d8-5b25-587b-82ab-31b8eaf6241c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295016} {:answer/id #uuid "7f755ae6-c7eb-51bf-94fc-266624fed2c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295017} {:answer/id #uuid "a2312ca5-eff4-553a-a644-b19dc0761f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295018} {:answer/id #uuid "e1be2f19-8cc5-5517-9d26-232e8e9b5564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2312ca5-eff4-553a-a644-b19dc0761f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295018} :answer-field/id #uuid "0282f857-c5ae-5e99-abd9-499df9ad1868" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b2687d59-41b8-5c66-a796-d05078f6e756" :question/math-academy-id "q-89889" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(1,4)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(1,4)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,4),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}-(4) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+6x+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(-{x}^{3}+6x+y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =-(1)^{3}+6(1)+(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(1,4).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,4).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
